<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wang Yanjun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/935">935</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Emperor Huizong of Min</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wang Yanjun was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-shenzhi">Wang Shenzhi</a>, founder of the Min kingdom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a>. He seized power in 926 and in 933 proclaimed himself emperor, breaking with the northern dynasties, but his extravagant rule ended when he was murdered in a palace coup in 935, the first of a series of killings among the Min rulers.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Wang Yanjun was the second biological son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-shenzhi">Wang Shenzhi</a> by his concubine Lady Huang; his birth date is unknown. He first appears in 917, when his father married him to Liu Hua, Princess Qingyuan of Southern Han, while he commanded his father's headquarters guards. After Wang Shenzhi's death in 925 his elder brother Wang Yanhan sent him out as prefect of Quan Prefecture. When he and his adoptive brother Wang Yanbing urged Wang Yanhan to stop gathering women for his palace, their relations broke down, and the two attacked the capital, Changle. Wang Yanbing took the city, killed Wang Yanhan and welcomed Wang Yanjun as acting military governor of Weiwu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-mingzong-of-later-tang">Emperor Mingzong of Later Tang</a> confirmed him as military governor and in 928 created him Prince of Min. That year he had 20,000 of his people become Buddhist monks. In 931 Wang Yanbing, hearing he was ill, attacked Fu Prefecture; Wang Yanjun's general Wang Renda killed his son Wang Jixiong in an ambush and captured him, and Wang Yanjun had him executed. Drawn to Taoist teachings on immortality, he was persuaded by the Taoist Chen Shouyuan to build the Emperor Bao Palace, and late in 931 he briefly became a Taoist monk under the name Xuanxi. When Later Tang ignored his demands for new titles, he cut off his tribute.</p><p>As emperor he took the name Wang Lin, built luxurious palaces against his frugal father's example and kept cordial relations with his neighbours. His chief of staff Xue Wenjie enriched him through false accusations and executions; after the plot of his nephew Wang Jitu more than 1,000 people were killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Late in life he was impotent after a stroke, and his favourite consort, Chen Jinfeng, whom he made empress in 935, had affairs with his associates Gui Shouming and Li Keyin. When the officer Li Fang had Li Keyin killed and Wang Yanjun ordered an inquiry, Li Fang led soldiers into the palace and wounded him, and palace servants killed him to end his suffering on 17 November 935. His son Wang Jipeng took the throne as Emperor Kangzong.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wang Yanjun” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His successors were less able: <b>Wang Yanjun</b> proclaimed himself emperor in 933, and later rulers murdered each other in a series of coups.</blockquote><a href="/state/min-ten-kingdoms">Min (Ten Kingdoms)</a></li></ul></section>
